      ],
      "infos": [
        {
          "card": [
            {
              "type": "header",
              "txt": "Welcome to pathology"
            },
            {
              "type": "content",
              "txt": "Our pathology services support patient care at Bagwan Health, by providing pathology testing focused on patient safety, clinical efficacy and quality reporting.\n\nPathology provides diagnostic, clinical and research services that focus on supporting both acute care and community health services.\n"
            },
            {
              "type": "row",
              "buttons": []
            }
          ],
          "rotateX": "0",
          "rotateY": "0"
        },
        {
          "card": [
            {
              "type": "header",
              "txt": "Specimen Reception"
            },
            {
              "type": "content",
              "txt": "Specimen reception is responsible for checking the details and samples.\nChecking that the correct sample has been received and that it meets all the essential criteria to be processed.\n\nAny preparation that is required for the samples is done; and the sample details are entered into the database by manual processes where we type all the details of the patient, the tests, and the sample dates.\n\nThen samples are distributed to the relevant disciplines.\n"
            },
            {
              "type": "row",
              "buttons": []
            }
          ],
          "rotateX": "0",
          "rotateY": "-45"
        },
        {
          "card": [
            {
              "type": "header",
              "txt": "Specimen Reception responsibilities"
            },
            {
              "type": "content",
              "txt": "Specimen reception is responsible for the initial stage of sample processing and laboratory. Samples are received from a variety of sources, including JPS, wards and outpatients in the hospital.\n\nSome samples may be urgent, which needs to be identified and prioritised to get those urgent requests to the relevant disciplines immediately.\n\nSamples types that can be received are blood, urine, sputum, swabs, and faeces. \n\nThe disciplines that we have here are biochemistry, haematology, blood transfusion, and microbiology.\n"
            },
            {
              "type": "row",
              "buttons": []
            }
          ],
          "rotateX": "0",
          "rotateY": "-70"
        },
        {
          "card": [
            {
              "type": "header",
              "txt": "Laboratory"
            },
            {
              "type": "content",
              "txt": "Seventy per cent of medical decisions rely on pathology tests. \n\nThe laboratory is a vital part of health care and often goes unnoticed, as the work is generally behind the scenes from patients.\n\nThe lab team can range extensively to include members who are pathologists, clinical lab scientists, medical lab technicians, phlebotomists and processers. \n\nAll who work together to determine either the absence, presence or the extent of disease found in patient samples.\n\nOnce samples are processed and tested, the results are provided to the nurses and doctors to help determine the diagnosis for the patient.\n"
            },
            {
              "type": "row",
              "buttons": []
            }
          ],
          "rotateX": "0",
          "rotateY": "90"
        },
        {
          "card": [
